        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433332",
          "text": "<p>Let $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ be a function such that $f(0)=0$ for some points but not for all points. Suppose that $f$ and its first derivative exist and are differentiable at all points. Suppose also that the second derivative $f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)=0$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.<br>Which of the following statements is true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 2 .</li><li>$f(x)$ is a constant function.</li><li>$f(x)$ is a linear function but not constant.</li><li>$f(x)$ is a cubic function.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433324",
          "text": "<p>Which is the Taylor series for the function $\\ln (x)$ at the point $a=1$ ?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{4}+\\cdots$</li><li>$(x-1)-(x-1)^{2}+2(x-1)^{3}-6(x-1)^{4}+\\cdots$</li><li>$\\ln (x)+\\frac{1}{x}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{x^{3}}(x-1)^{3}-\\frac{6}{x^{4}}(x-1)^{4}+\\cdots$</li><li>$\\ln (x)+\\frac{1}{x}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2 x^{2}}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3 x^{3}}(x-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4 x^{4}}(x-1)^{4}+\\cdots$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark,taylor-series",
          "category": "Engineering Mathematics"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433314",
          "text": "<p>The polynomial $P_{2}(x)=1+3(x-a)-2(x-a)^{2}$ is the second degree Taylor polynomial approximating the function $f$ for $x$ near $a$. The graph of $f$ is given in the figure. Which of the points A, B, C, or D on the $x$-axis has $a$ as its $x$-coordinate?<br><img alt=\"\" width=\"622\" height=\"200\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>A</li><li>B</li><li>C</li><li>D</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,two-marks",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433312",
          "text": "Given that the Taylor polynomial of degree four for $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is<br />$$<br />2-3 x+5 x^{3}+7 x^{4}<br />$$<br />what is the Taylor polynomial of degree five for $g(x)=\\frac{f\\left(x^{2}\\right)-2}{x}$ about $x=0$ ?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $5 x^{5}-3 x$</li><li> $-3 x+5 x^{3}-3 x^{5}$</li> <li>$5 x^{5}-3 x^{2}$</li>  <li>$5 x^{5}-3 x+2 x^{2}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,two-marks",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433318",
          "text": "<p>Let $f$ be a twice differentiable function at $x=3$. Let $P_{1}(x)$ be the local linearization of $f$ at $x=$ 3 and $P_{2}(x)=1-2(x-3)+2(x-3)^{2}$ be the second Taylor polynomial of $f$ at $x=3$.<br>Which of the following statements MUST be true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$P_{1}(x)=1-2(x-3)$</li><li>$f^{\\prime \\prime}(3)=2$</li><li>$f(3) &lt; P_1(3)$<p> </p></li><li>$f(3)=P_{1}(3)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,two-marks,multiple-selects",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433326",
          "text": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$. The second Taylor polynomial $p_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given. Find $p_{2}(3)$. (Choose the nearest answer.)<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> 1.1</li><li> 1.2</li> <li>1.3</li>  <li>1.5</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433325",
          "text": "Which of the following is NOT a Taylor series for $f(x)$ ?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $f(x+h)=f(x)+f^{\\prime}(x) h+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)}{2!} h^{2}+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}(x)}{3!} h^{3}+\\cdots$</li><li> $f(x)=f\\left(x_{0}\\right)+f^{\\prime}\\left(x_{0}\\right)\\left(x-x_{0}\\right)+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime}\\left(x_{0}\\right)}{2!}\\left(x-x_{0}\\right)^{2}+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}\\left(x_{0}\\right)}{3!}\\left(x-x_{0}\\right)^{3}+\\cdots$</li> <li>$f(x)=f(0)+f^{\\prime}(0) x+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime}(0)}{2!} x^{2}+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}(0)}{3!} x^{3}+\\cdots$</li>  <li>$f(x)=f\\left(x_{0}\\right)+f^{\\prime}\\left(x_{0}\\right) x+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime}\\left(x_{0}\\right)}{2!} x^{2}+\\frac{f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}\\left(x_{0}\\right)}{3!} x^{3}+\\cdots$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433328",
          "text": "Suppose the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ have Taylor series expansions at zero, up to second-degree terms, given by<br />$$<br />\\begin{aligned}<br />& f(x)=a_{0}+a_{1} x+a_{2} x^{2}+\\ldots \\\\<br />& g(x)=b_{0}+b_{1} x+b_{2} x^{2}+\\ldots<br />\\end{aligned}<br />$$<br />Find the Taylor series for $h(x)=f(x) g(x)$ at zero, up to second-degree terms.<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $h(x)=a_{0} b_{0}+\\left(a_{0} b_{1}+a_{1} b_{0}\\right) x+\\left(a_{0} b_{2}+a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{0}\\right) x^{2}+\\ldots$</li><li> $h(x)=a_{0} b_{0}+\\left(a_{0} b_{1}+a_{1} b_{1}\\right) x+\\left(a_{0} b_{2}+a_{2} b_{0}\\right) x^{2}+\\ldots$</li> <li>$h(x)=a_{0} b_{0}+\\left(a_{1} b_{0}+a_{0} b_{1}\\right) x+\\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{0} b_{2}\\right) x^{2}+\\ldots$</li>  <li>$h(x)=a_{0} b_{0}+\\left(a_{1} b_{0}+a_{0} b_{1}\\right) x+\\left(a_{1} b_{2}+a_{2} b_{0}\\right) x^{2}+\\ldots$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433330",
          "text": "<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" width=\"470\" height=\"163\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><p><br>Selected values of a function $f$ and its first three derivatives are indicated in the table above. What is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1$ ?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$2-3 x+\\frac{3}{2} x^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} x^{3}$</li><li>$2-3(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}$</li><li>$2-3(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3}$</li><li>$2-3(x-1)+3(x-1)^{2}-2(x-1)^{3}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433322",
          "text": "The function $f(x)$ is approximated near $x=0$ by the second-degree Taylor polynomial $T_{2}(x)=$ $5-7 x+8 x^{2}$. Which of the following statements is true?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $f(0)=5, f^{\\prime}(0)=-7, f^{\\prime \\prime}(0)=8, f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}(0)=0$</li><li> $f(0)=5, f^{\\prime}(0)=7, f^{\\prime \\prime}(0)=-8, f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}(0)=0$</li> <li>$f(0)=5, f^{\\prime}(0)=-7, f^{\\prime \\prime}(0)=16, f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}(0)=0$</li>  <li>$f(0)=5, f^{\\prime}(0)=7, f^{\\prime \\prime}(0)=8, f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}(0)=0$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433334",
          "text": "Consider the function $g(x)=2+x^{2}+\\frac{x}{\\left(4+x^{2}\\right)^{2}}$.<br />Find the degree 3 Taylor polynomial of $g(x)$ around $x=0$.<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $2+x^{2}-\\frac{x}{16}+\\frac{x^{3}}{32}$</li><li> $2+x^{2}+\\frac{x}{16}+\\frac{x^{3}}{32}$</li> <li>$2+x^{2}-\\frac{x}{16}-\\frac{x^{3}}{32}$</li>  <li>$2+x^{2}+\\frac{x}{16}-\\frac{x^{3}}{32}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433323",
          "text": "Let $T_{5}(x)=3 x^{2}-5 x^{3}+7 x^{4}+3 x^{5}$ be the fifth-degree Taylor polynomial for the function $f$ about $x=0$. What is the value of $f^{\\prime \\prime \\prime}(0)$ ?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> -30</li><li> -15</li> <li>-5</li>  <li>$-\\frac{5}{6}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433336",
          "text": "Let $f$ be a function having derivatives of all orders for all real numbers. The third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=2$ is given by:<br />$$<br />T(x)=7-9(x-2)^{2}-3(x-2)^{3}<br />$$<br />Which of the following statements are correct?<br /><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li> $f(2)=0$ and $f^{\\prime \\prime}(2)=-18$</li><li> $f(2)=7$ and $f^{\\prime \\prime}(2)=-9$</li> <li>$f(2)=7$ and $f^{\\prime \\prime}(2)=-18$</li>  <li>$f(2)=0$ and $f^{\\prime \\prime}(2)=-9$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,one-mark",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433316",
          "text": "<p>Let $f$ be a real-valued function defined on $\\mathbb{R}$. Suppose that $f$ has continuous first and second derivatives, and at some point $x=a$, the first derivative $f^{\\prime}(a)$ is zero, and the second derivative $f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)$ is positive for all $x$.<br>Which of the following statements is true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>If $x \\neq a$, then $f(x)=f(a)$.</li><li>If $x \\neq a$, then $f(x) &lt; f(a) $</li><li>If $x \\neq a$, then $f(x)&gt;f(a)$.</li><li>If $x \\neq a$, then $f(x) \\leq f(a)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,two-marks",
          "category": "Calculus"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433320",
          "text": "<p>The graph of $y=f(x)$ is given below. Assume that $f$ is infinitely differentiable everywhere. The Taylor series for $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by:<br>$$<br>f(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} c_{n} x^{n}=c_{0}+c_{1} x+c_{2} x^{2}+c_{3} x^{3}+\\cdots<br>$$<br><img alt=\"\" width=\"382\" height=\"395\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"><br>Based on the graph, determine the signs of $c_{0}, c_{1}$, and $c_{2}$ respectively:</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Negative, Positive, Positive</li><li>Positive, Negative, Negative</li><li>Zero, Positive, Negative</li><li>Negative, Zero, Positive</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-da-calculus-3,goclasses,calculus,two-marks",
          "category": "Calculus"
        }
      ]
    }
